adjudication.rationale:
In `reauthenticationOfUserWithoutPasskey`, the new test checks absence of the webauthn form via:
```java
try {
  MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
  fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
} catch (Exception nsee) {
  // expected
}
```
`driver.findElement(...)` never returns `null` (it returns a `WebElement` or throws), so this construct effectively asserts only that *some* `Exception` is thrown. Because it catches blanket `Exception`, the test will pass not only for the intended `NoSuchElementException`, but also for unrelated failures (e.g., `WebDriverException`, `StaleElementReferenceException`, etc.), masking real regressions such as page/rendering issues. Sibling tests in this diff use `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, ...)`, which would correctly distinguish expected absence from other failures.
